Q: what&#39;s wrong in this function? (beginner cs50) A: <p>The problem is somewhat subtle and a good example of why one should not hide pointers behind <code>typedef</code>s.</p>
<p>The type <code>string</code> is just a pointer to <code>char</code>.  In the function <code>replace</code> the <code>string</code> <code>final</code> is defined as</p>
<pre><code>    string final = a;
</code></pre>
<p>This does not make a copy of the string argument <code>a</code>, it just make <code>final</code> point to the same array. Hence when you iterate and compare the character at <code>a[i]</code>, find it in the alphabet and replace <code>final[i]</code> with the encoded character, <code>a[i]</code> points to the same character, so if you continue the loop the encoded character may be found again in the remaining part of the alphabet and get replaced again, etc.</p>
<p>You can fix this problem by breaking from the inner loop once you find the character:</p>
<p>Here is a modified version with the actual types and without an extra variable:</p>
<pre><code>char *replace(char *a, const char *b) {
    const char *alpha = &quot;abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz&quot;;
    for (int i = 0; a[i]; i++) {
        for (int n = 0; n &lt; 26; n++) {
            if (a[i] == alpha[n]) {
                a[i] = b[n];
                break;
            }
        }
    }
    return a;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note these remarks:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>replace</code> modifies the argument string in place, which may be confusing for many programmers.</li>
<li>only lowercase characters are replaced, which makes the cypher less effective.</li>
<li><code>main</code> should return <code>0</code> or <code>EXIT_SUCCESS</code> on successful operation.</li>
<li>if you can assume that letters are consecutive in the character set, which is the case for ASCII, you can simplify the code and handle both cases efficently:</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>#include &lt;cs50.h&gt;
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

char *replace(char *a, const char *b);

int main(int argc, string argv[])
{
    string code;
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf(&quot;Usage: ./substitution key\n&quot;);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    char *code = argv[1];
    if (strlen(code) != 26) {
        printf(&quot;Key must contain 26 characters.\n&quot;);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 26; i++) {
        unsigned char c = code[i];
        if (!isapha(c)) {
            printf(&quot;Key must only contain alphabetic characters.\n&quot;);
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; 26; j++) {
            if (code[i] == code[j]) {
                printf(&quot;Key must not contain repeated characters.\n&quot;);
                return EXIT_FAILURE;
            }
        }
    }
    char *txt = get_string(&quot;plaintext: &quot;);
    printf(&quot;ciphertext: %s\n&quot; , replace(txt, code));
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}

char *replace(char *a, const char *b)
{
    for (size_t i = 0; a[i]; i++) {
        unsigned char c = a[i];
        if (isupper(c)) {
            a[i] = (char)toupper(b[c - 'A']);
        } else
        if (islower(c)) {
            a[i] = (char)tolower(b[c - 'a']);
        }
    }
    return a;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Disable an option(s) in a dropdown Unity A: <p>Similar to <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/55298541/7111561">this answer</a> but a slightly different approach.</p>
<p>The other answer uses a hardcoded <code>toggle.name == &quot;Item 1: Option B&quot;</code> to compare the buttons. I'd rather use a central and index-based system:</p>
<p>Put this component on the <code>DropDown</code></p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>[RequireComponent(typeof(Dropdown))]
[DisallowMultipleComponent]
public class DropDownController : MonoBehaviour, IPointerClickHandler
{
    [Tooltip(&quot;Indexes that should be ignored. Indexes are 0 based.&quot;)]
    public List&lt;int&gt; indexesToDisable = new List&lt;int&gt;();

    private Dropdown _dropdown;

    private void Awake()
    {
        _dropdown = GetComponent&lt;Dropdown&gt;();
    }

    public void OnPointerClick(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        var dropDownList = GetComponentInChildren&lt;Canvas&gt;();
        if (!dropDownList) return;

        // If the dropdown was opened find the options toggles
        var toggles = dropDownList.GetComponentsInChildren&lt;Toggle&gt;(true);

        // the first item will always be a template item from the dropdown we have to ignore
        // so we start at one and all options indexes have to be 1 based
        for (var i = 1; i &lt; toggles.Length; i++)
        {
            // disable buttons if their 0-based index is in indexesToDisable
            // the first item will always be a template item from the dropdown
            // so in order to still have 0 based indexes for the options here we use i-1
            toggles[i].interactable = !indexesToDisable.Contains(i - 1);
        }
    }

    // Anytime change a value by index
    public void EnableOption(int index, bool enable)
    {
        if (index &lt; 1 || index &gt; _dropdown.options.Count)
        {
            Debug.LogWarning(&quot;Index out of range -&gt; ignored!&quot;, this);
            return;
        }

        if (enable)
        {
            // remove index from disabled list
            if (indexesToDisable.Contains(index)) indexesToDisable.Remove(index);
        }
        else
        {
            // add index to disabled list
            if (!indexesToDisable.Contains(index)) indexesToDisable.Add(index);
        }

        var dropDownList = GetComponentInChildren&lt;Canvas&gt;();

        // If this returns null than the Dropdown was closed
        if (!dropDownList) return;

        // If the dropdown was opened find the options toggles
        var toogles = dropDownList.GetComponentsInChildren&lt;Toggle&gt;(true);
        toogles[index].interactable = enable;
    }

    // Anytime change a value by string label
    public void EnableOption(string label, bool enable)
    {
        var index = _dropdown.options.FindIndex(o =&gt; string.Equals(o.text, label));

        // We need a 1-based index
        EnableOption(index + 1, enable);
    }
}
</code></pre>
